From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] sgdbgsock, mscoree: Wine Mono's debugger agent on the user's own Unix socket, never TCP

NET-DBG phase 1, David's choice (c). Wine Mono's soft debugger agent runs
whatever the debugger asks in the process, so only the user's own
processes may reach it. Its loopback TCP port (6074, opt-in) was reachable
by every local user.

sgdbgsock.dll (new, with a Unix side): a socket in the user's runtime
directory, $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/wine-mono-debug/NAME.sock -- the runtime
directory must be the user's and closed to others, the subdirectory is
made 0700, the socket 0600 -- opened in the calling process itself (not by
the wineserver, which a shared prefix shares between users); each end
takes a connection only from a process of the same uid (SO_PEERCRED), and
the socket's name goes once a debugger came. No runtime directory, no
socket: never one in a place others share. Exports SgDbgListen, Accept,
Connect, Send, Recv, Shutdown, Close for Wine Mono's dt_unix transport and
for debuggers.

mscoree: WINE_MONO_DEBUGGER_AGENT takes transport=dt_unix only (server,
suspend; server=n with the debugger's socket NAME); a server's socket is
named after the process id. dt_socket is refused -- and Wine Mono with the
transport (mono_sg_debugger_unix_transport) has no TCP transport at all.
A Wine Mono without it gets no agent.
